=== layout: subsection

# 26. Patents, Discovery, and Other Intellectual Property

=== layout: bookend

# 26. Questions

@body

- What is a patent?
- How would you distinguish between a patent and other forms of intellectual property (e.g., copyright, trademark, trade secrets)?
- How long does a patent last today, and what rights does it confer to the patent holder?
- What advantages do patents offer in science and industry?
- Discuss how patents encourage innovation by granting exclusivity and fostering investments.
- What are the criteria to be patentable?
- Explain the concepts of utility, novelty, and "non-obvious" with examples.
- What is the difference between discovery and invention?
- Provide general examples. Provide examples from biotechnology to illustrate each.
- What is copyright?
- How long does copyright last, and how does copyright compare with patents in terms of duration, scope, and nature of protection?
- When did copyright in the 1928 film Steamboat Willie expire in the United States, and how is that different from a patent or trademark?

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. What Are You Protecting?

@body

- A patent protects qualifying claimed inventions. Copyright protects original expression, not the underlying scientific fact.
- A trademark identifies the source of goods or services. A trade secret protects qualifying confidential information through secrecy rather than publication.
- One company can use several forms of protection at once.
- The name on the box, the text in the manual, the engineered process, and the confidential production settings are different things.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/patents/basics

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. A Patent Is a Right to Exclude

@body

- A U.S. patent can let its owner exclude others from specified acts involving the claimed invention, within the law's scope.
- It is not affirmative permission to sell the product, practice every necessary process, or ignore another owner's patent.
- Utility patents generally run 20 years from the relevant filing date, subject to rules including adjustments, extensions, and fees.
- The issued claims define the protected invention; an impressive title does not establish ownership of an entire field.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/patents/basics

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. An Invention Still Needs to Qualify

@body

- Patent eligibility is one question. Utility, novelty, nonobviousness, and adequate disclosure are additional requirements.
- Discovering a natural relationship can be scientifically important without making the relationship itself patentable.
- An engineered tool that uses the relationship may raise a different question, but invention is not an automatic stamp of patentability.
- Steamboat Willie concerns copyright in a film, not a Mickey Mouse patent. Its 1928 film entered the U.S. public domain in 2024; later works and trademarks are separate issues.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/patents/basics

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. Four Forms of Protection

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Tool | What It Protects | Biotechnology Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Patent | Qualifying claimed invention | Engineered production process |
| Copyright | Original expression | Manual, figure, or software code |
| Trademark | Source-identifying brand | Company or product mark |
| Trade secret | Qualifying secret information | Confidential production settings |

Copyright often lasts the author's life plus 70 years for qualifying modern works; other categories have different terms.

@citation

https://www.copyright.gov/help/faq/faq-duration.html

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. Why Disclose the Invention?

@body

- The patent bargain exchanges public technical disclosure for a limited right to exclude under the law.
- Protection can support investment in development, manufacturing, and commercialization. It can also limit access or increase the cost of follow-on work.
- Trade secrecy offers a different bargain: qualifying information remains protected while it remains secret, rather than being disclosed in exchange for a patent.
- Design patents concern qualifying ornamental designs; plant variety protection has its own requirements. These add to the four tools in the comparison.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/patents/basics

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. Discovery and Application

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Discovered Relationship | Engineered Application |
| --- | --- |
| Electrical phenomena | A particular electric lamp |
| Properties of steam | A particular steam engine |
| Biological sequence relationships | A particular molecular tool or process |

A valuable discovery can motivate an invention. A particular invention must still satisfy every applicable patent requirement.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: one-panel

# 26. Discovery or Invention?

@body

Choose A: *Discovery* or B: *Invention*. Then ask a harder question: would a specific claim satisfy all patent requirements?

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

*Topic 26: Discovery or Invention?*\
CRISPR bacterial immune system

- A. Discovery
- B. Invention

@answer

A. Discovery

Identifying a natural defense system\
adds knowledge about biology.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

*Topic 26: Discovery or Invention?*\
CRISPR/Cas9 gene-editing system

- A. Discovery
- B. Invention

@answer

B. Invention

An engineered tool applies biological knowledge;\
the precise claims still matter.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

*Topic 26: Discovery or Invention?*\
Gasoline-powered automobile

- A. Discovery
- B. Invention

@answer

B. Invention

An engineered machine applies known\
physical and chemical processes.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

*Topic 26: Discovery or Invention?*\
Energy from gasoline combustion

- A. Discovery
- B. Invention

@answer

A. Discovery

A natural physical and chemical relationship\
is not made patentable by discovering it.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

*Topic 26: Discovery or Invention?*\
Structure of a plant cell

- A. Discovery
- B. Invention

@answer

A. Discovery

Observing and describing a natural structure\
does not create that structure.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

*Topic 26: Discovery or Invention?*\
Pathogenic BRCA variants can increase cancer risk

- A. Discovery
- B. Invention

@answer

A. Discovery

The relationship is discovered.\
Do not describe ordinary BRCA function\
as simply causing cancer.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

*Topic 26: Discovery or Invention?*\
Introducing a drought-related gene into corn

- A. Discovery
- B. Invention

@answer

B. Invention

An engineered construct or organism may raise\
eligibility questions; inventiveness alone\
does not guarantee a patent.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2106.html

=== layout: subsection

# 27. Diamond V. Chakrabarty

=== layout: bookend

# 27. Questions

@body

- What new question did Diamond v. Chakrabarty raise about whether living things made by humans could be patented?
- How did Ananda Chakrabarty's oil-degrading bacterium differ from natural bacteria, and why was that difference important to the Court's decision?
- How did the U.S. Supreme Court decide that the engineered bacterium qualified as a "manufacture" or "composition of matter" under the Patent Act?
- How did the ruling change how scientists and companies thought about the value of genetically modified organisms?
- How did patent eligibility change the industry's view of engineered organisms?
- Why did the dissent want Congress to decide whether life could be patented?
- How did the ruling influence later patents on recombinant insulin, GM crops, and gene therapy?

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Living Was Not the Deciding Boundary

@body

- Chakrabarty developed a bacterium combining capabilities for breaking down components of crude oil.
- In 1980, the Supreme Court held that the human-made microorganism could be eligible subject matter under the patent statute.
- The key distinction was the claimed organism's human-made characteristics, rather than a rule that all life is unpatentable.
- Eligibility did not establish environmental safety or authorize a field release.

@citation

https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447303/usrep447303.pdf

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Two Sides of the Policy Debate

@body

- The majority read the statutory categories broadly enough to encompass the engineered microorganism.
- The dissent argued that Congress should decide whether the patent system should extend to such living organisms.
- Both positions concern the interpretation and reach of the statute. Neither determines that every genetically modified organism satisfies every patent requirement.
- Separate what a court held from broader opinions about whether patenting life is desirable.

@citation

https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447303/usrep447303.pdf

=== layout: one-panel

# 27. Return to the Oil-Degrading Microbe

@body

- The microorganism may be patent eligible, yet its developer still needs to address containment, environmental behavior, performance, and applicable regulatory oversight.
- A company could also need licenses to other claimed processes or tools.
- Ask three separate questions: can this invention qualify,\
  may the company practice it, and may the proposed product be used or sold this way?
- These questions connect patents back to the regulatory framework.

@citation

https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447303/usrep447303.pdf

=== layout: big-image

@image

![27. Three Questions for the Same Product](assets/set6_drawings/three_permissions.drawn.svg)

@caption

Patent eligibility and claims, freedom to operate, and regulatory authorization are separate questions.

=== layout: subsection

# 28. The Cohen-Boyer Recombinant DNA Patents

=== layout: bookend

# 28. Questions

@body

- How did Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer's recombinant DNA technique enable scientists to insert human genes into bacteria?
- What did their patent cover, and why was it so broad in scope?
- How did the University of California and Stanford manage licensing, and why did they choose a nonexclusive, low-cost model?
- How much revenue did these patents generate, and what impact did they have on early biotechnology startups?
- How did the Cohen-Boyer patents influence how future biotech inventions were shared, licensed, and commercialized?

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. The Process Was the Platform

@body

- Cohen, Boyer, and colleagues demonstrated methods for constructing recombinant plasmids and propagating introduced DNA in bacteria.
- The patent family concerned recombinant-DNA technology, not ownership of every natural gene.
- The application dates to 1974; patents issued later, beginning in 1980.
- Keep experiment, filing, patent issuance, and product approval as separate events on the timeline.

@citation

https://otl.stanford.edu/news/otls-first-50-years-executive-roundtable

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. Cut, Join, Transform, Select

@body

- Cohen, Chang, Boyer, and Helling's 1973 experiment joined restriction-enzyme fragments from different bacterial plasmids.
- Compatible ends allowed DNA fragments to be joined into recombinant plasmids.
- Transformation introduced the DNA into *E. coli*; selection identified bacteria carrying useful genetic markers.
- The introduced DNA could replicate and function in its new host. Making the recombinant molecule was only part of the result.
- Inserting a human coding sequence uses the platform; producing a functional protein adds expression and processing problems.

@citation

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC427208/

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. A Broad Licensing Strategy

@body

- Stanford managed licensing for the shared\
  Stanford/UCSF inventions using a broadly available, nonexclusive approach.
- A nonexclusive license allows multiple licensees to use covered technology under their agreements.
- Stanford reports about $255 million in licensing fees and royalties from the program.
- Revenue is one outcome. Broad access to a foundational research and production tool is another.

@citation

https://otl.stanford.edu/news/otls-first-50-years-executive-roundtable

=== layout: one-panel

# 28. Platform and Product Patents

@body

- A company using a covered cloning process could need a platform license as well as rights to a particular engineered construct or manufacturing method.
- The license's field, territory, term, and scope matter.
- Compare the incentives created by a broadly licensed platform with those created by an exclusive license for one narrow product.
- Which approach helps development, competition, and access in the specific setting?

@citation

https://otl.stanford.edu/news/otls-first-50-years-executive-roundtable

=== layout: subsection

# 29. Genentech, Recombinant Insulin, and Licensing

=== layout: bookend

# 29. Questions

@body

- How did Genentech's 1982 patent on recombinant insulin define ownership over producing human proteins using recombinant DNA methods?
- How did this patent lead to conflicts with other patent holders such as the University of California, which held related claims through the Cohen-Boyer patents?
- How did courts interpret the broad wording of Genentech's patent claims that covered using microbial hosts to make human proteins?
- How did these disputes shape the later concept of "freedom to operate" for biotechnology companies using recombinant DNA?
- How did Genentech's collaboration\
  with Eli Lilly influence how intellectual property was shared or licensed in early biotechnology?
- How did later court rulings limit the scope of broad biotechnology patents by emphasizing written description and enablement?
- How did the resolution of these patent conflicts shape how companies today protect biopharmaceutical inventions?

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. 1982 Is an Approval Milestone

@body

- Genentech helped demonstrate production of human insulin using recombinant DNA; Eli Lilly developed and commercialized Humulin.
- FDA approved Humulin in 1982, an important recombinant-medicine milestone.
- Do not identify 1982 as one universal Genentech patent date. A company can hold many patents with different filing dates, claims, owners, and terms.
- Approval, invention, licensing, and patent issuance are separate milestones.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history-exhibits/100-years-insulin

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. Build the Chains, Then Build Insulin

@body

- The early recombinant-insulin work used chemically synthesized DNA for the insulin A and B chains.
- Bacteria produced the chains as parts of larger fusion proteins.
- The researchers recovered the separate chains and joined them to form insulin.
- The 1979 paper demonstrated a production strategy; commercial manufacturing and the 1982 Humulin approval were further steps.
- A patent claim must identify the particular molecule, construct, or process being claimed.

@citation

https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/85300/

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. Several Contributions, One Product

@body

- A recombinant medicine can depend on cloning methods, expression constructs, host strains, fermentation, purification, formulation, and delivery.
- Different contributions can be owned or licensed separately.
- Owning an improvement does not automatically give freedom to practice an underlying patented technology.
- A collaboration can allocate development work and commercial rights without making every participant an owner of every underlying invention.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history-exhibits/100-years-insulin

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. Claims Must Match Disclosure

@body

- A broad patent claim needs adequate written description and enablement; naming a desired result is not the same as teaching how to achieve the claimed scope.
- Do not treat a claim to one demonstrated molecule as automatic ownership of all human proteins made in all microbial hosts.
- For your company's plan, identify the invention and the rights you would need.
- An intellectual-property milestone should be more specific than get a patent.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2163.html

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. A Concrete Insulin Patent Dispute

@body

- In *Regents of the University of California v. Eli Lilly* (1997), the court examined insulin-cDNA claims.
- Describing rat insulin cDNA did not adequately describe every claimed mammalian insulin cDNA or the claimed human sequence.
- A desired function is not automatically a description of the molecules that perform it.
- *Ariad v. Lilly* (2010) reaffirmed that written description is separate from enablement.
- Keep this dispute distinct from the Cohen-Boyer platform licenses and the UCSF/Genentech growth-hormone litigation.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2163.html

=== layout: one-panel

# 29. Do Not Merge Different Patent Stories

@body

- Recombinant insulin approval in 1982 and the Cohen-Boyer platform licenses are distinct historical events.
- A well-known UCSF/Genentech dispute concerned human growth hormone and techniques used to make Protropin. Contemporary UCSF reporting described that litigation in May 1999.
- That is not evidence for one universal 1982 insulin patent covering all recombinant proteins.
- For a disputed claim, name the patent, invention, parties, and proceeding before drawing a lesson about the whole industry.

@citation

https://synapse.ucsf.edu/articles/2019/05/21/date-ucsf-history-ucsf-genentech-battle

